# Salvage — configuration. `cp .env.example .env` and the app runs.
#
# The recovery itself needs nothing here. `engine.py` is standard library and
# fully offline: a broken .docx goes in, a valid one comes out, no key, no
# network. What this file configures is the OPTIONAL second pass — SuperDocs
# styling, which is what turns the plain rebuild into the file this product
# promises. With none of it set you still get a complete document and a
# sentence saying it was not styled.

# -- as it stands: the relay --------------------------------------------------
#
# Relay is a small deployed worker that holds a SuperDocs key and lends it out
# under a daily ration, so a fresh checkout styles documents without anybody
# signing up for anything first. RELAY_KEY is not a secret and is meant to be
# committed — it is the whole reason `cp .env.example .env` is enough.
#
# What it costs: 460 SuperDocs operations a day across everyone using it,
# 60 requests a minute, and an 8 MB ceiling on a single request. Operations
# are charged for document edits only — uploads, exports, job polls and
# session reads are free, so a recovery costs one and a change at the counter
# costs one. Budgets reset at 00:00 UTC.

RELAY_URL=https://relay.pxyz943.workers.dev
RELAY_KEY=pk_f2a01b7f189f0d1ea6c57a04cb83c14e

# -- or your own key ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Uncomment and fill this in and the relay stops being used entirely: requests
# go straight to SuperDocs' own origin, with no ration, no shared allowance,
# and no third party in the path. Your key is never sent to RELAY_URL — a key
# its owner brought goes to the origin and nowhere else.
#
# SUPERDOCS_API_KEY=sk_your_key_here
#
# And, if you are pointed at something other than the public origin:
# SUPERDOCS_BASE_URL=https://api.superdocs.app
